WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.690
When someone hands you a gift you never saw coming, why does a small unease stir inside you alongside the joy?

2
00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:11.460
Perhaps no gift is ever truly free; the bill simply arrives later.

3
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:17.190
In this episode, we ask whether gratitude is a virtue, or a debt that can never be fully repaid.

4
00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:27.280
Picture a wedding hall.

5
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:33.490
The lights are dimmed, the band has taken a break, and the bride and groom stand in the middle of the stage.

6
00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:36.340
In front of them stretches a long line.

7
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:44.950
Someone takes the microphone and calls out: "From the groom's uncle, one Republic gold coin!" The room bursts into applause.

8
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:52.440
Then the next: "From the bride's aunt, two quarter-gold coins!" The applause is a little thinner this time.

9
00:00:52.440 --> 00:01:02.170
Meanwhile, at a table off to the side of the stage, sits a cousin or a sister-in-law, usually the most meticulous member of the family, holding a squared notebook.

10
00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:07.740
Without looking up, she writes it all down: who gave what, and how many grams.

11
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.990
After the wedding, that notebook is tucked away in a drawer, and it is not forgotten.

12
00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:25.280
Years later, when the uncle's son is about to marry, the drawer is opened, the pages are leafed through, and the question is asked: "What did they give us?" The answer is certain, because it is written down.

13
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:33.890
There is even a subtle rule to the whole affair: give exactly the same, and you have settled your debt; give a little more, and now the other side owes you.

14
00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:37.220
Isn't there something strange about this?

15
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:45.190
A wedding is one of the days when people are most generous with one another, when the word "gift" is spoken more than at almost any other time.

16
00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:50.120
And yet the most carefully kept document of the entire night is a ledger.

17
00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:54.410
If a gift were truly a gift, why write it down?

18
00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:57.900
And if it is a debt, why do we call it a gift?

19
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.750
This question isn't unique to weddings.

20
00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:05.680
Say your neighbor sends over a plate of stuffed vine leaves.

21
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:15.250
In many homes, it is still considered rude to send that plate back empty; you put something on it, and if you have nothing to put on it, the plate waits in the kitchen.

22
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:21.300
During the holidays, you remember who called you, and perhaps you remember even better who didn't.

23
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:33.110
When a friend buys you an expensive present for your birthday, alongside the joy a small calculation begins inside you: When is her birthday, what will I get her, will I have to spend this much?

24
00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:36.120
No one does this arithmetic out loud.

25
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.290
If they did, the spell would be broken.

26
00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:44.540
But the calculation is there, pinned beneath every gift like an invisible invoice.

27
00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.030
That invoice has a name: obligation.

28
00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:55.360
And this is precisely our question: Is there such a thing as a free gift, or is every gift a debt that is expected to be repaid?

29
00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.690
Don't let the everyday nature of the question fool you.

30
00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:08.580
Two thousand years ago, a Roman philosopher wrestled with it; at the start of the last century, a French sociologist built an entire theory around it; and later,

31
00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.550
two philosophers tried to bring that theory crashing down from within.

32
00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:18.280
But first, let's look at just how old a tradition that squared notebook on the table belongs to.

33
00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:23.530
In the first century after Christ, a man named Lucius Annaeus Seneca lived in Rome.

34
00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:28.460
He was a Stoic philosopher, and also the tutor and advisor of the emperor Nero.

35
00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:34.830
In other words, he saw up close, at court, both what kindness is and what kindness costs.

36
00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:43.920
Seneca wrote the longest study of gift-giving that has come down to us from antiquity: a work in seven books called De Beneficiis, that is, On Benefits.

37
00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.770
Seneca opens the book with a complaint.

38
00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:51.540
People, he says, know neither how to give a kindness nor how to receive one.

39
00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:57.910
Some expect a return on what they give right away; others take what they are given and simply forget it.

40
00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:00.120
Then he builds an image.

41
00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:06.970
He recalls the three Charites of Greek mythology, the three goddesses of beauty whom the Romans called the Graces.

42
00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:12.220
In statues and paintings, these three maidens dance in a ring, hand in hand.

43
00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:20.350
Seneca passes on the interpretation of an earlier Stoic, Chrysippus: a kindness passes from hand to hand and ultimately returns to the giver.

44
00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:24.480
A break anywhere in the ring spoils the whole dance.

45
00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.890
What makes Seneca interesting is that he does not describe this ring as a form of trade.

46
00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:35.940
For him, what constitutes a kindness is not the object given, but the intention of the giver.

47
00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:41.510
Gold, a horse, a house — these are merely the outward signs of a kindness.

48
00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:46.760
The true kindness happens in the soul, and it lives on even if those objects are lost.

49
00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:56.330
This is why Seneca assigns different duties to each side, and he sums it up in one of the most frequently quoted lines of the book: let the one who gave the kindness be silent,

50
00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:59.500
and let the one who received it speak of it.

51
00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.150
The giver should forget; the receiver should remember.

52
00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:08.400
If a ledger is to be kept at all, it should be kept only in the heart of the one who received.

53
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.210
Some nineteen hundred years pass.

54
00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:21.460
In nineteen twenty-five, the French sociologist Marcel Mauss publishes a study titled Essai sur le don, that is, The Gift: An Essay.

55
00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:30.390
Mauss was an armchair anthropologist; he read field reports gathered from Polynesia, from Melanesia, and from the northwest coast of North America.

56
00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:34.840
Within this scattered material, he found a strikingly shared structure.

57
00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:39.130
According to Mauss, the gift is surrounded by three obligations.

58
00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:45.180
The first is the obligation to give: anyone who wishes to be respected in their community must give.

59
00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:53.310
The second is the obligation to receive: to refuse a gift that is offered means rejecting the relationship, even declaring hostility.

60
00:05:53.310 --> 00:06:01.280
The third is the obligation to reciprocate: a gift received must, after some time, be returned, and often with something added.

61
00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:08.290
The custom of never sending a plate back empty, the wedding notebook, the returning of a holiday visit...

62
00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:12.260
All of these are everyday forms of Mauss's three obligations.

63
00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:20.710
Asking where this obligation comes from, Mauss fixes on a concept handed down from the Maori, the Indigenous people of New Zealand: hau.

64
00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:27.880
As one Maori sage explained to an ethnographer, a gift holds within it a piece of the giver, a kind of spirit.

65
00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:31.210
This spirit does not want to stay with the object.

66
00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.900
It wants to return to where it came from.

67
00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:39.150
The gift you hold in your hands compels you, so to speak, to give something back.

68
00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:49.840
Anthropologists later argued over this interpretation at great length, but Mauss's intuition was clear: a gift stretches an unbroken thread between the one who gives and the one who receives.

69
00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:58.290
To show how taut this thread can become, Mauss describes the potlatch ceremonies of peoples such as the Kwakiutl of the American Northwest.

70
00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:07.540
At a potlatch, chiefs give one another gifts so enormous, and sometimes burn so much wealth in plain sight, that a rival who cannot match it loses face.

71
00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:10.870
Here generosity is more than a sign of friendship.

72
00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:13.400
It has also become a weapon.

73
00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.730
The side that cannot reciprocate is crushed.

74
00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:22.620
A year earlier, in a short essay, Mauss had captured this double nature with a play on words.

75
00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.270
In German, the word "Gift" means poison.

76
00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:31.360
In the old Germanic languages, the same root stood for both gift and poison.

77
00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:37.090
Mauss did not see this as a coincidence: a gift nourishes, but it also binds.

78
00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:45.380
For him, the gift was a "total social fact", a knot that takes in economics, law, religion and morality all at once.

79
00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:49.030
It was not free, but it was not trade either.

80
00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:53.960
It was a glue that held people together by keeping them in one another's debt.

81
00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:59.370
And so Seneca's dancing circle and Mauss's three obligations meet at the same point.

82
00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:03.980
A gift draws a circle, and that circle binds us to the community.

83
00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:08.430
But Mauss, too, had sensed that the circle has a darker side.

84
00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:14.480
What he saw in the potlatch was the first sign of it: sometimes a bond turns into a chain.

85
00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:17.970
Let us begin once more with an everyday scene.

86
00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:21.300
Imagine someone helped you through a hard time.

87
00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.750
Say they found you a job, or paid off your debt.

88
00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:27.800
Years go by.

89
00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:38.330
Then one evening, at a crowded dinner table, in front of everyone, they say with a laugh: "Where would this guy be now if it weren't for me, right?" The table laughs.

90
00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:41.660
You laugh too, but your face is burning.

91
00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:47.710
In that moment, the kindness they once did you has become something very different from what it was on the first day.

92
00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:54.400
Turkish has an idiom for exactly this: "başa kakmak", to hammer a favor into someone's head.

93
00:08:54.400 --> 00:09:00.130
The idiom itself suggests violence, because "kakmak" means to strike, to drive in.

94
00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:03.940
The kindness is driven into the receiver's head like a nail.

95
00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:09.990
This feeling is so old and so universal that religious texts answered it with an outright prohibition.

96
00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:19.720
In the two hundred sixty-fourth verse of the Quran's Surah al-Baqarah, believers are told: do not render your charity void by reminding others of it and by words that wound.

97
00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:28.650
The word "mann" in the verse means precisely this: reminding someone of what you gave them and expecting gratitude in return.

98
00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:34.060
In other words, rubbing a kindness in someone's face does more than make it ugly.

99
00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:36.430
It cancels it altogether.

100
00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:46.800
The hadith literature lists, among the seven who will be shaded on the Day of Judgment, the person who gives charity so secretly that their left hand does not know what their right hand has given.

101
00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:54.130
The same image appears in the Bible, in the Gospel of Matthew: let not your left hand know what your right hand is doing.

102
00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:57.300
Why was such a persistent warning needed?

103
00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:02.710
Because everyone knows what a kindness that is not kept quiet does to the person who receives it.

104
00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:12.120
In Thus Spoke Zarathustra, published between eighteen eighty-three and eighteen eighty-five, Friedrich Nietzsche makes an unsettling observation in a section devoted to the pitying:

105
00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:21.050
great debts do not make people grateful, they make them vengeful; and if a small kindness is not forgotten, it begins to gnaw inside them like a worm.

106
00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:27.580
At first hearing, what Nietzsche describes may sound immoral, but think for a moment about your own life.

107
00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:33.790
Do you always feel at ease around someone who has done you an enormous favor, one you can never repay?

108
00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:36.640
Sometimes we avoid that person.

109
00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:39.330
We put off answering their calls.

110
00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:43.140
We cross to the other side of the street when we see them coming.

111
00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.150
We do not do this because we hate them.

112
00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:50.120
We do it because their face reminds us of a bill we have not paid.

113
00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:54.890
In Nietzsche's eyes, the problem does not lie only with the receiver.

114
00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:57.740
The giver is often not innocent either.

115
00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:05.230
A hidden sense of superiority can lurk inside pity and charity: the helper stands above, the helped stands below.

116
00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:12.240
The saying "The giving hand is better than the receiving hand" was meant in the Islamic tradition to encourage generosity.

117
00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:18.290
But read the other way around, the same sentence also explains why the receiver feels crushed.

118
00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:25.780
What Mauss saw in the potlatch appears here in a quieter, more everyday form: a gift you cannot repay makes you smaller.

119
00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:31.350
The person who pulled this knot to its very end was the French philosopher Jacques Derrida.

120
00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:40.280
In his book Donner le temps, that is, Given Time, published in nineteen ninety-one, Derrida approaches Mauss with both respect and a sharp objection.

121
00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:47.770
He says that Mauss's book talks about everything except the gift: exchange, debt, reciprocity, economy.

122
00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:50.780
So what would a pure gift look like?

123
00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:53.310
Derrida's answer is dizzying.

124
00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:57.760
For a gift to truly be a gift, it must produce nothing in return.

125
00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:02.850
The moment the recipient says "thank you," a payment has been made.

126
00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:10.500
The moment the recipient realizes that it is a gift, a sense of debt is born within them, and the gift turns into an exchange.

127
00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:19.270
Even the moment the giver thinks to themselves, "what a good thing I've done," they have already collected their reward, in the form of that small pride in their own eyes.

128
00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:25.000
For Derrida, a gift can remain a gift only if it never appears as a gift to anyone at all.

129
00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:32.970
That is why he calls the gift "the impossible." The pure gift is something that vanishes the very moment we notice it exists.

130
00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:38.700
The closeness between Derrida's conclusion and the image of the right hand hidden from the left is striking.

131
00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:44.270
Both put a finger on the same wound: kindness that is seen issues its bill at once.

132
00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:48.720
Some societies have tried to give this problem an institutional solution.

133
00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:55.090
In Ottoman Istanbul, certain mosque courtyards and street corners held charity stones.

134
00:12:55.090 --> 00:13:00.180
These were stone columns about the height of a person, with a hollow carved into the top.

135
00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:06.070
A wealthy person, usually after darkness had fallen, would quietly leave money in the hollow.

136
00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:12.120
Later, someone in need would come, take only as much as they needed, and leave the rest for the next person.

137
00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:17.370
The giver never knew to whom they gave, and the receiver never knew from whom they received.

138
00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:23.420
There was no face to be reminded of the favor, no table to be avoided, no bill to be paid.

139
00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:26.910
And yet even here, Derrida would not give up.

140
00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:34.720
If the person who left money on the stone felt a sense of peace as they walked away in the dark, wouldn't that peace also count as a reward?

141
00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:37.250
Perhaps it would.

142
00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:40.420
Perhaps the pure gift really is impossible.

143
00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:44.550
But at this point, we may have overlooked something.

144
00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:53.000
Perhaps we have been asking the wrong question all along: perhaps the point is not to do away with gratitude, but to look at it in an entirely different way.

145
00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:59.530
There is a saying in Turkish that everyone knows: a single cup of coffee is remembered for forty years.

146
00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:04.620
Now look at how strange this saying is through the eyes of someone who keeps an account book.

147
00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:08.110
A cup of coffee is almost nothing.

148
00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:13.520
A few pennies' worth of beans, a little water, a ten-minute conversation.

149
00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:17.810
Written into a ledger, it would sit on the very bottom line.

150
00:14:17.810 --> 00:14:23.540
And yet the saying assigns this small offering a full forty years.

151
00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:25.910
What debt lasts forty years?

152
00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:27.640
None.

153
00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:32.090
Because what is at stake here is not a debt, but a bond of remembrance.

154
00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:37.980
The difference between this kind of remembrance and debt is the real knot of this episode.

155
00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:40.830
A debt wants to be settled.

156
00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:47.360
The moment it is paid, it ends, the entry is crossed out, and the two parties are free of each other.

157
00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:51.970
Remembrance, by contrast, does not want to be settled.

158
00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:56.260
It does not shrink as it is repaid; it deepens as it is remembered.

159
00:14:56.260 --> 00:15:02.470
What the person who offers the coffee wants is not to receive a cup of coffee every month for forty years.

160
00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:06.280
What they want is simply to be remembered, nothing more.

161
00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:13.770
Here we should also recall another Anatolian saying: dry onion eaten without obligation is better than honey eaten with obligation.

162
00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:18.860
At first glance, this saying and the one about coffee seem to contradict each other.

163
00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:24.110
One advises us to flee from obligation, the other to carry it for forty years.

164
00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:26.960
But in fact they speak of different things.

165
00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:35.410
The first advises us to flee from the kindness that is thrown back in our faces, the kindness that comes with a bill, the kindness that has turned into debt.

166
00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:42.420
The second advises us to protect the kindness that has not become debt, the kindness that has remained a bond of remembrance.

167
00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:49.590
In Islamic thought, one of those who drew this distinction most finely was al-Ghazali, who lived in the eleventh century.

168
00:15:49.590 --> 00:15:59.000
In the book on patience and gratitude within his great work, The Revival of the Religious Sciences, al-Ghazali describes gratitude as a structure with three layers.

169
00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:05.690
The first layer is knowledge: knowing from whom the blessing comes, and not mistaking it for something you are owed.

170
00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:10.780
The second layer is state: the joy that this knowledge gives rise to in the heart.

171
00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:17.470
The point al-Ghazali stresses is that this joy should be felt less for the blessing itself than for the will that brought it about.

172
00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:23.040
The third layer is action: using the blessing in a way that would please the one who gave it.

173
00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:26.210
There is something remarkable in this description.

174
00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:30.980
For al-Ghazali, gratitude is not a repayment but a way of seeing.

175
00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:36.870
When the true giver is God, there is nothing a human being could ever pay back in the first place.

176
00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:43.880
Yet this helplessness does not crush us; on the contrary, it frees us from the logic of debt.

177
00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:47.210
A debt that can never be paid cannot be invoiced.

178
00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:53.100
All that remains is to see, to rejoice, and to make good use of what we have received.

179
00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:59.630
In this sense, gratitude is not about closing an account, but about being able to see a blessing as a blessing.

180
00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:03.760
Seneca had arrived at a similar place two thousand years earlier.

181
00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:13.490
In the fourth book of On Benefits, he offers a surprising warning: the person who is too eager to repay a debt is an unwilling debtor, and an unwilling debtor is ungrateful.

182
00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:19.380
The one who rushes to give something back at once does not feel gratitude; they are trying to escape it.

183
00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:23.670
This is exactly where the shadow of the wedding ledger falls.

184
00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:34.360
When we answer a quarter-gold coin with a quarter-gold coin, and gold with gold, we close the account, but perhaps, in the very moment we close that ledger, we also lose something.

185
00:17:34.360 --> 00:17:39.770
We see this distinction at its most naked in the gifts we can never repay.

186
00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:48.540
A mother's sleepless nights, the patience of the teacher who taught you to read, a friend who called just to hear your voice during a hard year.

187
00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:53.310
These are never invoiced, and even if they were, they could never be paid.

188
00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:58.720
More often than not, we only grasp the size of what we received after the giver is gone.

189
00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:05.090
The person who offered that coffee is no longer alive, yet the forty years of remembrance still go on.

190
00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:10.340
The person we wish to thank is no longer here, but the thanks still lives within us.

191
00:18:10.340 --> 00:18:14.790
Perhaps this is exactly where gratitude flows in another direction.

192
00:18:14.790 --> 00:18:26.280
The debt we cannot repay to our mother is repaid to our child; the debt we cannot repay to our teacher is repaid to our student; the debt we cannot repay to a friend is repaid to a stranger we meet along the way.

193
00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:31.210
The gift does not return to the one who gave it, but it does not stop, either.

194
00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:39.980
We pass on what we have received, and so, instead of being settled like an invoice, it becomes a memory that carries on from generation to generation.

195
00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:43.310
We asked whether there is such a thing as a free gift.

196
00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:45.040
Perhaps there isn't.

197
00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:51.890
Perhaps beneath every gift lies an invisible invoice, and Derrida is right: the pure gift is impossible.

198
00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:54.740
But how we read that invoice is up to us.

199
00:18:54.740 --> 00:19:00.790
We can read it as a debt to be paid off and closed, or as a bond of goodwill to be carried for forty years.

200
00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:08.120
One day, when the checkered notebook from the wedding table is taken out of the drawer, perhaps no one will look at the figures inside.

201
00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:15.610
Perhaps, in those pages, between the tallies of grams of gold, they will see a list of the faces that once filled that hall.

202
00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:20.220
A list of people who touched our lives, many of whom are no longer with us.

203
00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:24.350
We have come to the end of today's episode of Neuron Philosophy.

204
00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:26.880
Until we meet again in a new story.
